            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "instant mixing",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "In the model, a stable annual cycle is usually established within 3 years, and model runs were therefore performed over 10 years with a daily time step. The model mixed layer has instant mixing, and the diffusivity below the mixed layer is constant, normally set to K\u2082 = 5.0\u00d710-6m\u00b2/s.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "1DICE",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "external forcing",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "A simpler setup of 1DICE is used here; a 1 m vertical resolution is used down to a representative Barents Sea depth, the atmosphere is purely external forcing.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "1DICE",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "ice thickness",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The 1DICE column model is forced by monthly mean observations and calculates the horizontally averaged ice thickness and the ocean column below. 1DICE was developed and described by Bj\u00f6rk (1989, 1997).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "1DICE",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "surface fluxes",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The 1DICE calculates surface fluxes based on standard bulk formulas, sea surface temperatures, and the forcing discussed in the next section.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "1DICE",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "heat",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "1DICE grows ice and mixes heat and salt/freshwater downwards.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "1DICE",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "salt",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "1DICE grows ice and mixes heat and salt/freshwater downwards.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "1DICE",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "freshwater",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "1DICE grows ice and mixes heat and salt/freshwater downwards.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Barents Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "It was early recognized that the Barents Sea climate is largely controlled by ocean heat transport (Mosby, 1962). Strong correlations between inflow and temperature and the large-scale atmospheric pattern upstream in the Norwegian Sea (the North Atlantic Oscillation) have also been well documented (Loeng et al., 1997; Dickson et al., 2000; Furevik, 2001).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Norwegian Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Strong correlations between inflow and temperature and the large-scale atmospheric pattern upstream in the Norwegian Sea (the North Atlantic Oscillation) have also been well documented (Loeng et al., 1997; Dickson et al., 2000; Furevik, 2001).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Norwegian Coastal Current",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Barents Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The net flow through the BSO based on observations is 2.0 Sv eastwards (1 Sv=1\u00d7106 m\u00b3/s). This net flow is derived from four sources; the inflowing Norwegian Coastal Current (NCC) at the southern coast, the Atlantic inflow in the central area, and two westward flows in the northern areas (Fig. 1).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Bear Island Current",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Barents Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Finally, 0.9 Sv leaves in the Bear Island Trench (Skagseth, 2008), and another ~0.3 Sv in the shallow Bear Island Current (Blindheim, 1989). Thus, there is an inflow of 3.2 Sv and an outflow of 1.2 Sv giving a net eastward flow of 2.0 Sv.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
